        <Document ID="34EFA1B7-786F-414B-825D-843CAB3B67E0">
            <Title>Program Structure</Title>
            <Text>Program Structure

Because there's really no Tektronix 405X IDE available, you'll want to do program design and organization in some kind of external document. You can party like it's 1979 by using a legal pad (or some kind of engineering notebook) and a pencil, or maybe a text file on a computer.  Keeping track of what the program is about, and what sections of line numbers belong to whatever logical components that you're using.

Traditionally programs start at line 100. This is s nice round line number that skips over all of the User-Definable key definitions from lines 4 through 84. You have three lines of code at the very start that you can use for a GOTO 100, maybe set up an interrupt handler or two first-thing.

Some ideas - using the tape append to load common routines.
This could also be a good place to talk about the renumbering features
</Text>
            <Notes>maybe have a diagram with the the initial trampoline, the user-definable keys, and then the rest of the program.  Perhaps with subroutines sketched out.
</Notes>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="5641A8C0-884F-4C39-9C47-EB7BFACB73F1">
            <Title>Yay Languages</Title>
            <Text>Notes on programming languages, with a particular emphasis on the classic languages and implementations.

Each language has its own folder which has the generic blurbage, and inside of that are documents for each specific implementation (e.g. VAX FORTRAN or Tektronix BASIC), and maybe subdocuments for better organization. 
</Text>
        </Document>
        <Document ID="02D28D17-9900-4F4A-B1EC-FE9266F19B13">
            <Title>Tektronix</Title>
            <Text>Commands are unique in their first three characters. (not sure if that's officially stated, or just from inspection, but the overview commands show three letters)

The docs are kind of cool - two syntax diagrams, one with abbrevations and type of argument "string, numeric, string, string", and then a second one with what the arguments represent "blood type, weight, shoe size, dog's maiden name"
</Text>
        </Document>
